Full-file review complete (2841 lines, all read). I verified the Lua scoping claims against declaration order via grep line numbers.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 1173,
    "summary": "emergencyStop() never clears finalizationPending or stops the _whisperFinalize timers, so a pending final transcription still fires and inserts text after the user hit Emergency Stop.",
    "failure_scenario": "User releases the trigger key → stopRecording() sets finalizationPending=true and arms the 0.3s timer. Within that 0.3s the user clicks 'Emergency Stop' (menu bar) → emergencyStop() sets isRecording=false, kills whisper-cli, force-hides the overlay and notifies 'Stopped'. The 0.3s timer then fires, sees finalizationPending still true, and runs doFinalTranscription() → the overlay reappears, whisper runs, and the text is pasted into whatever app is now focused. Emergency stop does not stop anything."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 804,
    "summary": "`isRecording` inside createOverlay's mouseCallback resolves to the global (nil), not the local declared at line 853, so the overlay X button never calls emergencyStop() while recording.",
    "failure_scenario": "createOverlay is defined at line 719; `local isRecording = false` is declared at line 853, i.e. after it, so the closure cannot capture it and compiles to a _ENV global lookup that is always nil. Clicking the X mid-recording takes the `else` branch: `overlay:delete()`. ffmpeg keeps writing chunks, pulseTimer/clockTimer keep ticking, and the recording continues invisibly until the key is released. This is precisely the 'X button not closing overlay' symptom documented in AGENTS.md."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 822,
    "summary": "`hideOverlay()` called from createOverlay's mouseCallback is a nil global — `local function hideOverlay` is declared at line 835, after createOverlay's body ends at 826.",
    "failure_scenario": "User clicks the overlay background twice (pin, then unpin). Line 815-822 runs: the global `isRecording` is nil so `not isRecording` is true, and it calls the global `hideOverlay`, which was never assigned → 'attempt to call a nil value (global hideOverlay)' is thrown inside the canvas mouse callback. The overlay never hides and an error is logged on every unpin click."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 367,
    "summary": "getChunkFiles() sorts ffmpeg's `chunk_%03d.wav` filenames lexicographically, which reorders the audio once the recording exceeds 999 chunks (~16m40s).",
    "failure_scenario": "ffmpeg is started with `-segment_time 1` and `chunk_%03d.wav` (line 1634); past 999 it emits chunk_1000.wav, chunk_1001.wav, … `table.sort` compares strings, so '.../chunk_1000.wav' < '.../chunk_999.wav' ('1' < '9'). A 20-minute dictation is assembled as chunks 000-099, then 1000-1200, then 100-999 — the final transcript comes out with the last four minutes spliced into the middle. The code explicitly supports long recordings (barMaxSecs auto-expands in 3-minute steps, FINAL_SEGMENT_SECS splitting), so this range is reachable in normal use."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 1329,
    "summary": "`hs.sound.getByFile(...):play()` is still unguarded at three call sites (1329, 1657, 1722) even though commit 30a76bf added a nil guard for the identical call at 1798; at 1329 the throw aborts finishInsertion before hideOverlay is scheduled.",
    "failure_scenario": "getByFile returns nil (sound file missing/unreadable, or the AudioToolbox load fails). At line 1329 `nil:play()` throws, so line 1330's `hs.timer.doAfter(OVERLAY_LINGER, hideOverlay)` never runs and the overlay is stuck on screen indefinitely after every dictation. At 1657 the same throw aborts pollForFirstChunk; at 1722 it aborts the warmup-failure path so the 'Микрофон недоступен' overlay never hides either. The fix should be one shared playSound(name) helper, not a pcall bolted onto a single call site."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 1035,
    "summary": "`overlayPinned` is written as a local at line 1035 but read as a global everywhere else (lines 803-841 all precede `local overlayPinned` at line 854), so pinning the overlay from the menu bar has no effect.",
    "failure_scenario": "User picks 'Settings...' from the menu bar → line 1035 assigns the local `overlayPinned = true`. hideOverlay() at line 836 reads the *global* overlayPinned, which is still false, so the next dictation's `hs.timer.doAfter(OVERLAY_LINGER, hideOverlay)` tears down the pinned settings overlay. The local at 854 is write-only dead state: nothing after line 854 ever reads it."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 131,
    "summary": "hasOllama() runs a synchronous, un-timeout-ed `os.execute` curl on the Hammerspoon main thread, and buildMenuBarMenu() calls it unconditionally on every menu open.",
    "failure_scenario": "Ollama is listening on 11434 but wedged (loading a large model, or the process is stopped). `curl -s -o /dev/null http://localhost:11434/api/tags` has no --max-time and blocks. Because Hammerspoon is single-threaded, the whole app freezes — the modTap eventtap stops delivering flagsChanged, so the dictation trigger key goes dead until curl gives up. Triggered by simply clicking the menu bar icon (line 991), or by any dictation ≥50 chars with refine on (line 300). Add `--max-time 1` at minimum, or cache the probe result."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 2479,
    "summary": "emitText strips bracketed noise markers with the greedy pattern `%[.*%]`, which deletes everything between the first `[` and the last `]` — real transcript text included.",
    "failure_scenario": "A meeting window transcribes to \"[BLANK_AUDIO] we agreed on the budget [typing] and Bob will follow up\". `raw:gsub(\"%[.*%]\", \"\")` matches from the first `[` through the last `]` and yields \" and Bob will follow up\" — the budget decision is silently dropped from both the live notepad and the saved markdown. The pattern must be lazy: `%[.-%]`."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 1735,
    "summary": "The finalization-recovery branch in startRecording() returns after doFinalTranscription() without starting the recording the user just asked for, contradicting its own log message.",
    "failure_scenario": "User releases the key and immediately re-presses within the 0.3s finalization window (or presses after a sleep/wake where finalizationPending survived). startRecording() takes the recovery branch, logs 'resuming finalization before starting a new recording', calls doFinalTranscription() and returns — no warmup, no ffmpeg. The user holds the key with no overlay and no red dot; on release the eventtap's `not triggered and (isRecording or isWarmingUp)` guard at line 1834 is false so stopRecording() is never called either. The whole keypress is silently swallowed and the user must press again."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 2687,
    "summary": "startMeeting ignores switchToAggregateOutput()'s boolean return, so the meeting records with no system audio when the aggregate cannot be built.",
    "failure_scenario": "BlackHole is present (hasBlackHole passes) but `runAudioHelper(\"recreate\")` fails — helper binary is stale, or the user is stranded on the aggregate with no audible fallback. switchToAggregateOutput() logs and returns false; startMeeting proceeds anyway, shows the notepad, and starts ffmpeg reading from a BlackHole device that no system audio is being routed to. The user records a whole meeting and gets back a transcript containing only their own microphone (or silence), with no error surfaced anywhere but the log."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 1244,
    "summary": "doPartialTranscribe's onPartialText takes (text, errMsg) but transcribeViaAPI invokes its callback as (text, detectedLang, errMsg), so in API mode every success is reported as an error and every real error is swallowed.",
    "failure_scenario": "In API mode transcribeViaAPI's success path calls `callback(decoded.text, normalizeApiLang(decoded.language), nil)`. onPartialText binds errMsg to the language code, which is truthy, so it renders 'API error: en' in the overlay and logs 'partial: en' — the transcribed text is never displayed. The failure path calls `callback(\"\", nil, errMsg)`, so errMsg binds to nil and the real error is dropped entirely. (Currently latent: partialTimer is commented out at line 1643, so doPartialTranscribe has no caller — the ~70-line function plus partialTimer/partialBusy/lastChunkCount teardown at 1176/1763 is dead weight that should either be wired back up with the correct arity or deleted.)"
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 1103,
    "summary": "updateProgressBar derives `elapsed` from recordingStartTime, which is never reset when recording stops, so barMaxSecs keeps inflating during transcription and the blue completion bar can never reach 100%.",
    "failure_scenario": "After stopRecording, onSegmentText calls updateProgressBar once per segment. `elapsed = now - recordingStartTime` keeps climbing, repeatedly tripping `elapsed >= barMaxSecs * 0.9` and adding 180s to barMaxSecs on each call. The red 'recording' bar therefore keeps advancing after the user let go of the key, and finishAll's `transcribedSecs = barMaxSecs; updateProgressBar()` (line 1427) recomputes with a barMaxSecs that just grew again, so txnFrac < 1.0 and the 'flash to 100% to confirm all audio was transcribed' never actually reaches 100%."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 2701,
    "summary": "getBlackHoleDevice() may return nil, and a nil in the middle of the ffmpeg args table constructor silently truncates the array rather than erroring.",
    "failure_scenario": "hasBlackHole() checks via hs.audiodevice.findInputByName while getBlackHoleDevice() scans allInputDevices(); if they disagree (device removed between the two calls, or a name variant), bhDevice is nil. `{ \"-y\", \"-f\", \"avfoundation\", \"-i\", nil }` gives an args table of length 3, so the subsequent table.insert calls append after '-i' is gone. ffmpeg is launched with no `-i` at all, exits immediately, and the meeting records zero bytes — the only signal is 'meeting: ffmpeg exited 1' in the log while the notepad cheerfully says 'Recording from BlackHole 2ch...'."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 860,
    "summary": "`_whisperFinalize` is introduced as a bare global to dodge the 200-local ceiling, violating the AGENTS.md rule that new state be grouped into an existing table.",
    "failure_scenario": "AGENTS.md states: \"**Rule**: Never add a new top-level `local` without first verifying the count stays ≤ 200. If at or near 200, group into an existing table or create a new one\" and lists PL as the table holding pipeline state including `watchdog`. Line 860 instead leaks a global into Hammerspoon's shared _ENV (where any other Hammerspoon config or Spoon can collide with it), and line 859 spends one of the last two remaining local slots (`grep -c '^local '` reports 199/200) on `finalizationPending`. Both belong in `PL` alongside the existing `watchdog` field, which would cost zero locals and zero globals."
  },
  {
    "file": "hammerspoon/init.lua",
    "line": 1209,
    "summary": "doPartialTranscribe opens the concat list without a nil check and immediately writes to it, unlike the equivalent site at line 1451 which does check.",
    "failure_scenario": "If $TMPDIR/whisper-dictate is unwritable or has been cleaned out from under the process, `io.open(batchList, \"w\")` returns nil and line 1211's `f:write(...)` throws 'attempt to index a nil value (local f)'. partialBusy is left true, so partial transcription is wedged for the remainder of the session. Line 1451 already handles this correctly with `local f, ferr = io.open(...)` plus a guard; mirror that here."
  }
]
```
